text:
Except for egyptian blue, not known at that time
...
&quot;Egyptian blue—the world&#x27;s oldest known synthetic pigment, created nearly 5,000 years ago and lost to history after the Roman Empire—has been systematically recreated by modern researchers and historians.&quot;

Do people miss the word &quot;ergonomically&quot; in my original comment? Because yeah, the semantics is essentially the same, just as it would be the same for e.g.<p><pre><code>    double.TryParse(&quot;5.x5&quot;, out double val, out bool ok);
    if (ok) { ... }
</code></pre>
in case of a hypothetical variant of C# that doesn&#x27;t have functions, only procedures, so you&#x27;d have to return the data from subroutine calls by ref&#x2F;out&#x2F;globals exclusively. It&#x27;s still would be the same semantics — but quite different ergonomics.<p>So out of those three choices: return all results by assigning to ref&#x2F;out parameters; return one result directly, all other by assigning to ref&#x2F;out parameters; return all results directly — I personally prefer the last one. Almost nobody prefers the first one, yet people seem to be strangely attached to the second variant for some reason.

&gt; Your target is the Arch User.
&gt;  
&gt; Specifically: the recently stabilized Arch user.<p>Okay, but what about the veteran Arch user?
